🔹 1. Callable (Task that returns result)

Callable is an interface used to define a task that returns a value.

Key points:
Introduced in Java 5 (java.util.concurrent)
Similar to Runnable, but:
✅ returns a result
✅ can throw checked exceptions
Method:
V call() throws Exception;
Example:
Callable<Integer> task = () -> {
    return 10 + 20;
};
🔹 2. Future (Result holder of async task)

Future represents the result of an asynchronous computation.

It is returned when you submit a Callable (or Runnable) to an Executor.

Key methods:
get()        // get result (blocking)
isDone()     // check if completed
cancel()     // cancel task
isCancelled()
🔹 3. How they work together

👉 Callable = task
👉 Future = result of that task

Example:
ExecutorService executor = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(2);

Callable<Integer> task = () -> {
    Thread.sleep(1000);
    return 50;
};

Future<Integer> future = executor.submit(task);

// Do something else...

Integer result = future.get(); // blocks until result is ready
System.out.println(result);

executor.shutdown();
🔹 4. Runnable vs Callable (important)
| Feature      | Runnable        | Callable    |
| ------------ | --------------- | ----------- |
| Return value | ❌ No            | ✅ Yes       |
| Exception    | ❌ No checked    | ✅ Yes       |
| Method       | `run()`         | `call()`    |
| Use case     | Fire-and-forget | Need result |

🔹 5. Callable + Future flow (interview explanation)
Create a Callable
Submit it to ExecutorService
Get a Future
Call future.get() to retrieve result
🔹 6. Important interview points
future.get() is blocking
Use future.get(timeout, unit) to avoid waiting forever
Multiple tasks → use invokeAll()
For better async chaining → use CompletableFuture (modern approach)
🔹 7. When to use what?
Use Runnable → when you don’t care about result
Use Callable + Future → when you need result from thread
Use CompletableFuture → for advanced async programming
🔹 One-line answer (for interview)

Callable defines a task that returns a result, while Future is used to retrieve the result of that asynchronous task.